{"name":"Inner Space","hex":"#285b5f","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"inner","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Being or occurring (farther) inside, situated farther in, located (situated) or happening on the inside of something, situated within or farther within contained within something.","Close to the centre, located near or closer to center."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An inner part.","A duvet, excluding the cover."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/inner"},{"term":"space","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Unlimited or generalized extent, physical or otherwise.","A physical extent across two or three dimensions (sometimes for or to do something)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To roam, walk, wander.","To set some distance apart."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/space"}],"entries":[{"title":"Destination Inner Space","blocks":[{"text":"Destination Inner Space is a 1966 science fiction film produced by Earl Lyon, directed by Francis D. Lyon, written by Arthur C. Pierce, and stars Scott Brady, Gary Merrill, and Sheree North. The film was released to theaters in the US in May 1966 on a double bill with Frozen Alive (1964); its broadcasting rights were pre-sold to television so that some of the licensing fee could be used to finance the film's production. The story centers on scientists working in a laboratory on the floor of the ocean. They encounter an undersea flying saucer, after which the lab is attacked by a colorful aquatic humanoid monster who they fear may be the first in an alien invasion."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/c/cc/Destination_Inner_Space_poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Destination_Inner_Space","wikidata":"Q5265137"},{"title":"Inner space (science fiction)","blocks":[{"text":"Inner space in the context of science fiction refers to works of psychological science fiction that emphasize internal, mental, and emotional experiences over external adventure or technological speculation, which contrasts with traditional science fiction's fascination with outer space."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inner_space_(science_fiction)","wikidata":"Q66123325"},{"title":"Space","blocks":[{"text":"Space is a three-dimensional continuum containing positions and directions. In classical physics, physical space is often conceived in three linear dimensions. Modern physicists usually consider it, with time, to be part of a boundless four-dimensional continuum known as spacetime. The concept of space is considered to be of fundamental importance to an understanding of the physical universe. However, disagreement continues between philosophers over whether it is itself an entity, a relationship between entities, or part of a conceptual framework."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/3D_coordinate_system.svg/330px-3D_coordinate_system.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Space","wikidata":"Q107"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/c/cc/Destination_Inner_Space_poster.jpg/250px-Destination_Inner_Space_poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/c/cc/Destination_Inner_Space_poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/c/cc/Destination_Inner_Space_poster.jpg/250px-Destination_Inner_Space_poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADestination_Inner_Space_poster.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Gottfried_Wilhelm_Leibniz%2C_Bernhard_Christoph_Francke.jpg/500px-Gottfri